 ### On-the-Fly Distance Field & Hypertexture Lookup
-A variant where the distance field is derived from the mesh on the fly on the GPU, using the surface vicinity for hypertexture lookups [S3, unofficial]. It aims for very high quality, using up to 20 octaves of Perlin noise for shading and complex materials [S3, unofficial]. Performance is limited by complex GPU computations, achieving around 20 FPS for a $1024^3$ volume [S3, unofficial].
+A method where a distance field is derived from the mesh on the fly on the GPU, and surface vicinity is used for hypertexture lookups [S3, unofficial]. It uses 20 octaves of Perlin noise for shading and complex materials [S3, unofficial]. Due to complex GPU computations, frame rate is around 20 FPS for a $1024^3$ volume [S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Time complexity is high due to the evaluation of multiple noise octaves and the base SDF at every ray step [S2]. On-the-fly variants can reach ~20 FPS for $1024^3$ volumes [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory complexity depends on the noise lattice and octave storage [S3, unofficial]. On-the-fly GPU implementations may use compacted textures (e.g., RGBA32) to store node indices, with memory usage scaling with the node pool size (e.g., 4 MB for $64^3$ entries) [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** The method adds significant geometric detail without increasing polygon count and is compatible with sphere tracing for efficient ray marching [S1, unofficial][S2].
-*   **Limitations:** The approach requires careful Lipschitz constant calculation to avoid ray marching artifacts and incurs high computational cost for high-resolution or high-octave implementations [S2][S3, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** The technique is recommended when organic, detailed surface relief is needed without the cost of high-poly geometry or explicit mesh generation [S1, unofficial].
+- **Time Complexity:** High computational cost due to multiple noise evaluations and octaves. Frame rates can be low (e.g., 20 FPS for $1024^3$ volume) [S3, unofficial].
+- **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the number of octaves and the size of the Gamma array. In streaming implementations, memory usage can be optimized using node pools and bricks [S3, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** Adds high-frequency detail without increasing geometric complexity; enables sphere tracing for efficient rendering [S1, unofficial][S2].
+- **Limitations:** Computationally expensive; requires careful handling of Lipschitz constants for stable sphere tracing [S2].
+- **When to use:** When high-quality procedural detail is needed on implicit surfaces and performance allows for complex GPU computations [S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The concept of hypertextures was proposed by Perlin [PH89] as a method for procedural texture synthesis evaluated within a volumetric region around an object [S1, unofficial]. It was inspired by local ray-casting methods for volume data but applied to surface relief [S1, unofficial]. The specific formulation for sphere tracing with distance bounds was detailed in subsequent work on implicit surfaces and noise deformation [S2].
+Proposed by Perlin [PH89], hypertextures were inspired by the idea of evaluating procedural texture synthesis within a volumetric region around an object rather than only on the surface [S1, unofficial]. This allows for the modeling of phenomena intermediate between surface and texture [S1, unofficial]. The specific variant affecting the range to enable sphere tracing was detailed in subsequent work [S2].
 
